                                                            
● 🏕️  Your Scout Prompt Generator - Explained Simply!                                                                                                                                                      
                                                            
  The Big Picture                                                                                                                                                                                         
                                                                                                                                                                                                          
  Think of your app like a restaurant kitchen where:
  - Ingredients = Your scout data (skills, badges, requirements)                                                                                                                                          
  - Recipe = The form you fill out                          
  - Chef = The prompt generator
  - Meal = The final prompt you copy and use with AI

  ---
  📂 Folder Structure (The Kitchen Layout)

  scout-prompt-generator/
  ├── app/                    # The main dining area (what users see)
  │   ├── page.tsx           # Home page - the main entrance
  │   └── layout.tsx         # Restaurant layout (fonts, colors)
  │
  ├── components/            # Reusable building blocks (like Lego pieces)
  │   ├── layout/           # Header & Footer
  │   ├── program-planner/  # Form components
  │   └── ui/               # Basic pieces (buttons, cards, inputs)
  │
  ├── lib/                  # The kitchen utilities
  │   ├── types.ts         # Recipe cards (data shapes)
  │   ├── prompt-generator.ts  # The master chef
  │   ├── adventure-skills.ts   # Skill database
  │   └── activity-badges.ts    # Badge database
  │
  └── public/              # Static files (like photos on the wall)
      └── data/           # Your JSON data files

  ---
  🔄 How It Works (The Assembly Line)

  1. User Opens the App (app/page.tsx)

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
  │           HEADER                    │  ← Header component
  ├─────────────────────────────────────┤
  │                                     │
  │  Welcome to Scout Prompt Generator  │
  │                                     │
  ├──────────────┬──────────────────────┤
  │              │                      │
  │   LEFT SIDE  │    RIGHT SIDE        │
  │              │                      │
  │  [FORM]      │   [PREVIEW]          │
  │              │                      │
  │  Step 1...   │   Live updating      │
  │  Step 2...   │   prompt text        │
  │  Step 3...   │                      │
  │  Step 4...   │   [Copy Button]      │
  │              │                      │
  └──────────────┴──────────────────────┘
  │           FOOTER                    │
  └─────────────────────────────────────┘

  2. User Fills the Form (components/program-planner/ProgramForm.tsx)

  The form has 4 steps (like a multi-page survey):

  Step 1: Section & Duration
    ├─ Scout Section (Beavers/Cubs/Scouts/Ventures/Rovers)
    ├─ Program Duration (weeks)
    ├─ Meeting Duration (minutes)
    └─ Group Size

  Step 2: Program Focus
    ├─ Program Type (Skill/Badge/Theme)
    ├─ If "Skill": Select skill & stage
    ├─ If "Badge": Select activity badge
    └─ If "Theme": Enter custom theme

  Step 3: Location & Context
    ├─ Location
    ├─ Environment (indoor/outdoor/mixed)
    └─ Season (optional)

  Step 4: Additional Options
    └─ Include weekend activity?

  3. Data Flows to Preview (The "Node" Approach)

  // In page.tsx - The Brain

  // 1. Store form data (like a clipboard)
  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({...})

  // 2. Store generated prompt (like a document)
  const [generatedPrompt, setGeneratedPrompt] = useState('')

  // 3. Watch for changes (like a security camera)
  useEffect(() => {
    // Whenever formData changes, wait 300ms
    // Then generate new prompt
    generatePrompt(formData)
  }, [formData])

  Visual Flow:
  User types in form
          ↓
  formData updates (reacts to change)
          ↓
  300ms delay (debounce - prevents too many updates)
          ↓
  generatePrompt() runs (the chef starts cooking)
          ↓
  generatedPrompt updates (the meal is ready)
          ↓
  Preview shows new text (served to customer)

  4. Prompt Generation (lib/prompt-generator.ts)

  This is like a mad libs template filler:

  async function generatePrompt(options) {
    let sections = []

    // Add header
    sections.push(buildHeader(options))

    // Add program details
    sections.push(buildProgramDetails(options))

    // Add skill/badge requirements
    if (options.programType === 'skill') {
      sections.push(buildCompetenciesSection(...))
    }

    // Add SPICES (fixed text)
    sections.push(buildSpicesSection())

    // Add location context
    sections.push(buildContextSection(options))

    // Join all sections with newlines
    return sections.join('\n\n')
  }

  What it produces:
  You are an experienced scout leader...

  PROGRAMME DETAILS:
  - Section: Scouts (Age: 12-15 years)
  - Duration: 6 weeks
  ...

  ADVENTURE SKILL COMPETENCIES: Camping - Stage 3
  • Pitch and strike a tent
     - Set up a tent properly in 5 minutes
  ...

  SPICES FOCUS: social, physical, intellectual...
  ...

  ---
  🧩 The Component Tree (Node Approach)

  Think of components as nested boxes (like Russian dolls):

  <Home>                     # Root component (the whole house)
    ├─ <Header>             # Room: Header
    │    └─ <nav>           # Furniture: Navigation
    │
    ├─ <main>               # Room: Main content
    │    ├─ <ProgramForm>   # Furniture: Form cabinet
    │    │    ├─ <Card>     # Drawer: Step 1 card
    │    │    ├─ <Card>     # Drawer: Step 2 card
    │    │    ├─ <Card>     # Drawer: Step 3 card
    │    │    └─ <Card>     # Drawer: Step 4 card
    │    │         └─ <Select>  # Utensil: Dropdown
    │    │         └─ <Input>   # Utensil: Text field
    │    │
    │    └─ <PromptPreview> # Furniture: Preview panel
    │         └─ <Card>     # Frame: Preview card
    │              └─ <Button>  # Utensil: Copy button
    │
    └─ <Footer>             # Room: Footer

  Each component is:
  - Independent - Can be used anywhere
  - Reusable - Use <Button> in many places
  - Props-driven - Receives data from parent
  - State-aware - Can manage its own data

