  // This means:
  <div className="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6">

  // On mobile:  1 column (stacked)
  // On desktop: 2 columns (side by side)
  // Gap: 6 units between columns

  Responsive Design:
  Mobile (< 1024px):      Desktop (≥ 1024px):
  ┌──────────────┐        ┌──────────┬──────────┐
  │   Form       │        │  Form    │  Preview │
  │              │        │          │          │
  │   Preview    │        │          │          │
  └──────────────┘        └──────────┴──────────┘
     (stacked)                (side by side)

  ---
  🚀 What Happens When User Types?

  1. User types "Dublin" in location field
  2. Input component fires onChange event
  3. ProgramForm calls updateField('location', 'Dublin')
  4. page.tsx receives new data via setFormData()
  5. useEffect detects formData changed
  6. Waits 300ms (debounce - saves processing)
  7. Calls generatePrompt() with new data
  8. PromptPreview receives new prompt via props
  9. Screen updates with new text

  All in milliseconds! ⚡

  ---
  💡 Why This Architecture?

  ✅ Modular - Each piece can be changed independently
  ✅ Reusable - Use Button component everywhere
  ✅ Maintainable - Easy to find and fix bugs
  ✅ Scalable - Easy to add new features
  ✅ Type-safe - TypeScript prevents mistakes

  ---
  📝 Summary

  Your app is a data processing pipeline:

  JSON Data → Form → State → Prompt Generator → Preview → User Copies

  The "node approach" means breaking everything into small, reusable components that pass data between them like a relay race. Each component does one job well, and they work together seamlessly! 🎯


